{#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  THE CHOICE — the switch that /cookies promised and did not have
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  For years the only way to refuse arrival counting on this site was to send `DNT`
  or `Sec-GPC`. Chrome removed the Do Not Track setting and Safari removed it before
  that, so for most of this platform's visitors — Chrome on an Android phone — there
  was no way to say no at all, while the same page promised "you will be asked
  before it runs".

  So this is the part that has to work with nothing else: no JavaScript, no
  Alpine, no fetch. It is a plain form with two submit buttons and it posts. A
  privacy control that needs scripts to run is a privacy control that is missing
  for exactly the people most likely to have switched scripts off.

  ── WHY TWO BUTTONS AND NOT A TOGGLE ────────────────────────────────────────

  Because a toggle has to be read before it can be used — you have to work out
  which way is on — and because the accessible name of a switch changes meaning
  depending on its state, which is the one control type screen-reader users most
  often report having to test by flipping. Two buttons each say what pressing them
  does. The current state is stated above them in words, once, and the button for
  the state you are already in is not drawn: pressing it would do nothing, and a
  dead control is worse than an absent one.

  ── THE STATE IS SERVER-RENDERED ────────────────────────────────────────────

  `ag_privacy` is HttpOnly, so nothing here could read it even if it wanted to.
  That is deliberate: handing every page's scripts a privacy preference to read
  adds a fingerprinting surface to save a round trip we are not making.
#}
{% set c = cookie_control %}

<style nonce="{{ csp_nonce }}">
  .ck{border:1px solid #d7dcdb;background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:1.4rem 1.5rem;margin:0 0 2rem}
  .ck__kicker{font:600 11px/1 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;letter-spacing:.14em;
              text-transform:uppercase;color:#6b7472;margin:0 0 .75rem}
  .ck__state{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:.6rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:0 0 .45rem}
  .ck__now{font:700 1.22rem/1.25 Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;color:#16211f;margin:0}
  /* The one colour on the card, and it earns it: this chip is the whole answer.
     Emerald is the house action colour and is reserved — counting that is running
     is not an action, so the "on" state takes the gold accent and the refused
     state takes a plain slate. Nothing here should read as a warning: refusing is
     not an error and must not be dressed as one. */
  .ck__chip{font:600 11px/1 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;letter-spacing:.1em;
            text-transform:uppercase;padding:.38rem .6rem;border-radius:999px;white-space:nowrap}
  .ck__chip--on{background:#fdf3d6;color:#7a5a05;border:1px solid #f3b416}
  .ck__chip--off{background:#eef1f1;color:#41504d;border:1px solid #ccd4d2}
  .ck__why{font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.6;color:#44514f;margin:0 0 1.1rem;max-width:60ch}
  .ck__why:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
  .ck__act{display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center}
  .ck__btn{font:600 .95rem/1 inherit;padding:.72rem 1.15rem;border-radius:10px;cursor:pointer;
           border:1px solid #237b22;background:#237b22;color:#fff}
  .ck__btn:hover{background:#1c661b;border-color:#1c661b}
  .ck__btn--quiet{background:#fff;color:#16211f;border-color:#cfd6d4}
  .ck__btn--quiet:hover{background:#f0f2f2}
  .ck__btn:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #f3b416;outline-offset:2px}
  .ck__note{font-size:.86rem;color:#6b7472;margin:1rem 0 0;max-width:62ch}
  .ck__saved{font:600 .9rem/1.4 inherit;color:#1c661b;margin:0 0 .9rem}
  @media (max-width:520px){ .ck__act{flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch} }
</style>

<section class="ck" id="your-choice" aria-labelledby="ck-h">
  <p class="ck__kicker">Your choice</p>

  {% if c.saved %}
    {# Shown once, on the redirect after a press. A control that changes nothing you
       can see is a control people press twice. #}
    <p class="ck__saved" role="status">Saved. {{ c.saved_word }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="ck__state">
    <h2 class="ck__now" id="ck-h">{{ c.headline }}</h2>
    <span class="ck__chip ck__chip--{{ c.counting ? 'on' : 'off' }}">
      {{ c.counting ? 'Counting this visit' : 'Not counting' }}
    </span>
  </div>

  <p class="ck__why">{{ c.explanation }}</p>

  {% if c.locked %}
    {# Their browser said no. Drawing a "yes" button here would be offering to
       overrule them, and we have decided not to — so we say so instead of
       pretending the choice is open. #}
    <p class="ck__note">{{ c.locked_note }}</p>
  {% else %}
    <form method="post" action="/cookies/choice" class="ck__act">
      <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token }}">
      {% if c.counting %}
        <button type="submit" name="choice" value="no" class="ck__btn">Do not count my visits</button>
        <a class="ck__btn ck__btn--quiet" href="#counting-arrivals" style="text-decoration:none">What is counted?</a>
      {% else %}
        <button type="submit" name="choice" value="yes" class="ck__btn">Count my visits</button>
        <a class="ck__btn ck__btn--quiet" href="#counting-arrivals" style="text-decoration:none">What would be counted?</a>
      {% endif %}
    </form>
  {% endif %}

  <p class="ck__note">
    Remembering your answer needs one cookie of its own, <code>{{ c.cookie_name }}</code>. It holds a
    single character &mdash; a yes or a no &mdash; and no identifier of any kind. It is the record of
    your decision, so it is the one thing here we cannot offer to remove.
  </p>
</section>
